INT. MOBILE HOME - KITCHEN TABLE - DAY

A narrow kitchen. Wood paneling. A window unit rattles. On the table: a stack of papers, a stamp pad, and a coffee mug that says WORLD'S OKAYEST NOTARY.

BEATRIX LOMAX-DIALLO, 60s, notary, reading glasses on her forehead, sits at the head. To her left, HENRY TAKAHASHI, 70s, widower, rests both hands flat. To her right, his son-in-law, DANTE VELARDE, 40s, in a pressed shirt, fidgets with a pen.

She stamps a page. He watches the stamp.

He reaches for another page. She slides it toward him.

BEATRIX
This one transfers the deed.

HENRY
I know what it does.

DANTE
Henry, take your time.

HENRY
I've taken my time. I'm seventy-three.

Dante nods. He lowers his eyes. Beatrix clears her throat.

BEATRIX
Mr. Velarde, you'll sign as witness.

DANTE
Of course.

She slides the pen to him. He takes it. His hand shakes slightly. He signs. She notarizes.

BEATRIX (CONT'D)
Mr. Takahashi, you are conveying the property to your daughter Keiko's estate, correct?

HENRY
Keiko's gone.

BEATRIX
I'm sorry.

HENRY
To her children, then.

DANTE
They're minors.

BEATRIX
A trustee, then.

HENRY
(looking at Dante)
You.

Dante pauses. He sets down the pen.

DANTE
Henry, I don't need to be trustee.

HENRY
You'll do it right.

Beatrix looks from one to the other. She taps the stamp. She lifts the final page. He holds out his hand. She places the page in it.

He reads. Slow. Every line. Then he signs, large and square, and slides it back.

She stamps. The sound is small, dry, final.

BEATRIX
Congratulations.

HENRY
Is that what it is?

She smiles, gently, and pours him more coffee.
